rain</article-title><source>Nat. Rev. Neurosci.</source><year>2006</year><volume>7</volume><fpage>30</fpage><lpage>40</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16371948</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b21"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Campbell</surname><given-names>LW</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Aging changes in voltage-gated calcium currents in hippocampal CA1 neurons</article-title><source>J. Neurosci.</source><year>1996</year><volume>16</volume><fpage>6286</fpage><lpage>6295</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8815908</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b22"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Carafoli</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Calcium signaling: a tale for all seasons</article-title><source>Proc. Natl Acad. Sci. USA</source><year>2002</year><volume>99</volume><fpage>1115</fpage><lpage>1122</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11830654</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b23"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Chan</surname><given-names>SL</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Presenilin-1 mutations sensitize neurons to DNA damage-induced death by a mechanism involving perturbed calcium homeostasis and activation of calpains and caspase-12</article-title><source>Neurobiol. Dis.</source><year>2002</year><volume>11</volume><fpage>2</fpage><lpage>19</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12460542</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b24"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Chan</surname><given-names>SL</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Presenilin-1 mutations increase levels of ryanodine receptors and calcium release in PC12 cells and cortical neurons</article-title><source>J. Biol. Chem.</source><year>2000</year><volume>275</volume><fpage>18195</fpage><lpage>18200</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10764737</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b25"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Chavis</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Functional coupling between ryanodine receptors and L-type calcium channels in neurons</article-title><source>Nature</source><year>1996</year><volume>382</volume><fpage>719</fpage><lpage>722</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8751443</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b26"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Cheng</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The exit from G(0) into the cell cycle requires and is controlled by sarco(endo)plasmic reticulum Ca<sup>2+</sup> pump</article-title><source>Arch. Biochem. Biophys.</source><year>1996</year><volume>329</volume><fpage>65</fpage><lpage>72</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8619636</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b27"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Choi</surname><given-names>DW</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Excitotoxic cell death</article-title><source>J. Neurobiol</source><year>1992</year><volume>23</volume><fpage>1261</fpage><lpage>1276</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">1361523</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b28"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Choi</surname><given-names>DW</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Glutamate neurotoxicity in cortical cell culture</article-title><source>J. Neurosci.</source><year>1987</year><volume>7</volume><fpage>357</fpage><lpage>368</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">2880937</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b29"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Clodfelter</surname><given-names>GV</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Sustained Ca<sup>2+</sup>